Here's a complete example of an MCP server wrapping a REST API:

---

## Tool Class — `ApiTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a user by their unique ID.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The numeric user ID")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var response = await httpClient.GetAsync($"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Search users by name. Returns matching user records.")]
    public async Task<string> SearchUsers(
        [Description("Name or partial name to search for")] string query,
        [Description("Max results to return (1-50)")] int limit = 10,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Searching users: {Query}", query);
        var response = await httpClient.GetAsync(
            $"/users?name={Uri.EscapeDataString(query)}&limit={limit}", cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

---

## `Program.cs` — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for the API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK has two injection styles:

**1. Constructor injection (non-static class, shown above):**
The class is instantiated per-request by the DI container. Services registered in `Program.cs` (like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`) are injected via the primary constructor. This is the standard .NET DI pattern.

**2. Method parameter injection (static class):**
DI services can appear directly as method parameters — the SDK resolves them automatically and **excludes them from the tool schema** exposed to the LLM.

```csharp
[McpServerToolType]
public static class StaticTools
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Ping the API.")]
    public static async Task<string> Ping(
        HttpClient httpClient,           // injected by DI, hidden from schema
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        return await httpClient.GetStringAsync("/health", cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

**Key rules:**
- `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>`, and any DI-registered service are **never** exposed in the tool schema
- Only parameters with `[Description]` are visible to the LLM
- Use `.WithToolsFromAssembly()` to auto-discover all `[McpServerToolType]` classes via reflection

This gives you clean separation: the LLM sees only the semantic parameters, while your tools get full access to infrastructure services.
